---
// The fourth tool page. One URL, indexable, its own title and prose — people arrive here from
// a search for "split pdf", not from the landing page.
//
// The interactive part is one island. Everything above and below it is static, so the page is
// readable and its claim is checkable with JavaScript switched off.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { PRESENT_MARK, STRIP_WITHDRAWN, WITHDRAWN_MARK } from "../components/strip-copy.js";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Split PDF — cut it into parts on your own computer | Not Only PDF"
  description="Cut a PDF into separate documents. The splitting happens in your browser and the file is never uploaded, because there is no server to upload it to."
>
  <div class="read">
    <h1>Split PDF</h1>

    <p>
      Cut a PDF into separate files — take a chapter out of a report, or break a long scan into the
      documents it should have been. The cutting happens in this browser, on this computer: the file
      is not uploaded, because Not Only PDF has no server that could receive it.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    /*
    NO `client:*` DIRECTIVE. Astro's hydration directives bootstrap an island with an INLINE
    script, and `script-src 'self'` carries no `'unsafe-inline'` and no nonce (ADR 0014) — so
    the browser refuses the bootstrap, the island never hydrates, and the tool renders as dead
    markup with nothing visible but a CSP line in the console. Measured on `/merge-pdf`; the
    same wall, the same answer on every page since.

    The ENGINES are still not loaded here. The tool starts a worker the first time a file is
    chosen (ADR 0018). Mounting the component is a few kilobytes; starting the engines is
    about 420 KB over the wire (1.8 MB uncompressed) --- it was 6.8 MB until spike 0004 took
    PDFium out of the payload.
  */
  }
  <div id="split-tool"></div>

  <script>
    import { type Component, mount } from "svelte";

    import SplitTool from "../components/SplitTool.svelte";

    const target = document.getElementById("split-tool");
    if (target) {
      // The cast is Astro's type, not ours: `@astrojs/svelte` declares a `.svelte` default
      // export as an ASTRO component, because that is what a `client:*` directive would need.
      // Mounting it by hand is the same module reached through Svelte's own API.
      mount(SplitTool as unknown as Component, { target });
    }
  </script>

  <noscript>
    <div class="read no-js">
      <h2>This tool needs JavaScript</h2>
      <p>
        The cutting is done by code running in your browser, so with JavaScript switched off there
        is nothing to do the work. There is no server-side version to fall back to — that is the
        whole design, not a missing feature.
      </p>
      <p>
        With JavaScript on, this page lets you choose a PDF, say where to cut it, and download each
        part. Nothing is sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </noscript>

  <div class="read">
    <h2>Saying where to cut</h2>

    <p>
      Give the pages you want to cut <strong>after</strong>. A cut goes in the gap that follows a
      page, so on a ten-page document <code>3, 7</code> makes three files: pages 1-3, 4-7 and 8-10. Cutting
      after page 10 is refused, because the part after it would have no pages in it.
    </p>

    <p>
      A range cuts after each page in it, so <code>1-9</code> on a ten-page document gives every page
      its own file — and the <strong>Cut after every page</strong> button types that for you. It types
      it into the box rather than switching to a mode, so you can see what it asked for and change one
      cut afterwards.
    </p>

    <p>
      The page shows the files you will get before you run anything. That is there because
      &ldquo;after&rdquo; is the part people read past, and showing the result is more honest than
      asking you to take our word for how it was read.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each file is named for the pages inside it — <code>report-pages-04-07.pdf</code> — and the numbers
      are padded so that ten parts sort in order rather than 1, 10, 2. The name is not a label we attach
      afterwards: it is the pages that file contains.
    </p>

    {
      STRIP_WITHDRAWN ? (
        <p>
          <strong>{WITHDRAWN_MARK}.</strong> They were here briefly and were withdrawn: drawing a
          page needs a second PDF engine in the same tab as the one doing the work, and holding both
          could cost you the tab in the middle of an operation. Choosing pages by number is how this
          page worked before the pictures existed, and it is what it does now. They come back when
          the second engine no longer has to be resident while a splitte runs.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          <strong>{PRESENT_MARK} once you choose a file.</strong> They are drawn on your computer by
          the same code that does the splitting, and each one is drawn when you scroll to it rather
          than all at once. A page too complex to draw quickly says so and is skipped; the cuts
          still work by number, which is how this page worked before there were pictures at all.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <details>
      <summary><h2>What happens to large files</h2></summary>

      <p>
        Everything is held in this browser tab's memory, so the practical limit is what your device
        can spare rather than an upload size. Not Only PDF refuses a file larger than 512 MB before
        it opens it, and a document of more than 10,000 pages before it cuts any of it — so an
        oversized job is refused rather than failing part way through.
      </p>

      <p>
        What that does not save you from, said here rather than discovered: a very large document
        that is under both limits can still make the tab slow while it runs, and on a phone the
        browser may stop the tab entirely. That is the device's memory ceiling, reached inside the
        PDF engine where nothing can intervene.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Nothing is handed to you until every part has been made and checked.</strong> A split
        that fails on the ninth of ten parts gives you nothing at all, deliberately: the point of splitting
        is that the parts add up to the document you started with, and nine files out of ten do not add
        up to anything. If it stops part way, you still have your original and no half-finished set to
        sort through.
      </p>
    </details>

    {
      /*
      OPEN BY DEFAULT, AND ONLY ON THIS PAGE. Every other disclosure on every other tool page
      starts closed. This one does not, because what is inside it is not general reassurance:
      ADR 0019 section 4 names the wording /split-pdf is held to, and it is the explanation of
      why a part cannot carry data belonging to the pages it excluded. That is the promise
      that lifted split's hold (issue #54). A promise a person has to click to find is a
      weaker promise than the one the ADR describes, so the disclosure keeps the section
      scrollable rather than hiding it.
    */
    }
    <details open>
      <summary><h2>What is kept, and what is not</h2></summary>

      <p>
        <strong>Each part contains its pages exactly as they were</strong> — their contents, their size
        and their annotations. <strong
          >Links keep working when they point inside the same part</strong
        >, and links to a page in a different part are removed rather than left to go nowhere. Web
        links are kept.
      </p>

      <p>
        Form fields keep their appearance but are no longer fillable. What makes a field a field is
        recorded for the document as a whole, not on the page, so it cannot come with one part — and
        carrying it would carry the names and the values typed on pages that part does not contain.
        Bookmarks and attached files are left out for the same reason: they describe the whole
        document, and copying them into every part would put the names of pages you did not include
        into files you did.
      </p>

      <p>
        A document that uses layers cannot be split here at all. Whether a layer is hidden is
        recorded for the document as a whole, so Not Only PDF has no way to find out — and a hidden
        layer that arrived visible would be worse than a refusal.
      </p>

      <p>
        A file that is encrypted, damaged, or not a PDF is refused. So is a cut that would produce
        an empty file — cutting after the last page, or after the same page twice.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The no-JS block is ordinary content, but it only ever appears in place of the tool, so it
     gets the same top margin the tool has. */
  .no-js {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }
</style>
